===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|scho}}, {{m|enm|sho}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sċōh||shoe}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*skōh}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*skōhaz||shoe}}, of unclear etymology; possibly a derivation from {{m|gem-pro|*skehaną||to move quickly}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*skek-||to move quickly, jump}}.

Eclipsed non-native {{noncog|enm|sabatine}}, {{m|enm|sabatoun|t=shoe}} from {{noncog|ML.|sabatēnum}}, {{m|la|sabatum|t=shoe, slipper}} (compare {{noncog|pro|sabatō}}, {{noncog|es|zapato|t=shoe}}, {{noncog|fr|sabot|t=wooden shoe, clog}}, {{noncog|it|ciabatta}}).

The archaic plural ''[[shoon]]'' is from {{inh|en|enm|shon}}, from {{der|en|ang|scōn}}, {{m|ang|scōum|t=shoes|pos=dative plural}} and {{m|ang|scōna|t=shoes'|pos=genitive plural}}; it is cognate with {{cog|sco|shuin|t=shoes}}.

{{rel-top|Cognates:}}
See also {{cog|sco|shae}}, {{cog|fy|skoech}}, {{cog|nds|Schoh}}, {{cog|nl|schoen}}, {{cog|de|Schuh}}, {{cog|bar|Schuach}}, {{cog|da|-}}, {{cog|no|-}} and {{cog|sv|sko}}, {{cog|txb|skak|skāk|balcony}}. {{rel-bottom}}

